# Patterns match paths RELATIVE TO THE CONTEXT ROOT, so a rule written without
# `**/` is anchored to the root and matches nowhere else. A bare `.env` excluded
# only the one beside this file — the second live .env under .claude/worktrees/
# went into every image. `**/` is what makes a rule mean "at any depth".
# `.env.*` also drops .env.example: it documents a deploy, nothing here reads it.
**/.env
**/.env.*

# Nothing below is a build input — the whole surviving context was checked and
# contains none of them. They are here so the next secret to land in the tree
# leaves by default, rather than repeating the .env lesson in a new shape.
**/.envrc
**/.npmrc
**/.netrc
**/.git-credentials
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/id_rsa*
**/id_ed25519*

**/node_modules
**/.next
**/dist
**/*.tsbuildinfo
**/.git

# Owner-only, and none of it runs: an agent worktree (a whole second copy of this
# repo, carrying its own .git, runs/ and .env), the build record, the design
# record, README media.
.claude
.superpowers
docs
assets
inspiration

# Anchored at the two scratch directories rather than at `public/`, which Next.js
# serves: naming the folder itself would silently drop the first real static asset
# anyone adds. Today both children are all there is, so a clean checkout has no
# public/ at all — Next treats it as optional, and these rules are the insurance.
packages/web/public/canvases
packages/web/public/graph-review

# Root-anchored ON PURPOSE. `**/runs` would also match packages/web/app/runs/,
# the route that lists runs. The worktree's copy leaves with .claude above.
runs
